Keep going when a party lies, instead of naming it and stopping.

Against MP-SPDZ 0.4.3 (6 Jul 2026). One new header and two changed ones.
Enabled with `--options robust`; without it `atlas-party.x` is unchanged.

What this is for
----------------
`locate-inconsistent-shares.patch` made the engine name the party that sent a
wrong share. It still stopped. `ACCOUNTABILITY.md` calls that rung 4 and is
explicit that rung 5 --- the honest parties get the output whatever the corrupt
ones do --- needs more than a decoder bolted onto a reconstruction.

It needs less than Goyal-Song-Zhu, though, and reading the engine says why.

Where the shortcut comes from
-----------------------------
A product before degree reduction is a degree-`2t` codeword over `n` points, and
Reed-Solomon corrects `e` errors iff `n - d >= 2e + 1`. Correcting `t` of them
therefore needs

    n - 2t >= 2t + 1,  i.e.  n >= 4t + 1.

At `n = 7, T = 2` the capacity is 1 and this deployment is exactly one short. At
`n = 9` it is 2. That is the entire reason for nine nodes.

`t < n/3` is the line where segmenting, checkpoints and player elimination
become necessary. `t < n/4` is the line where a decoder is enough.

Why it lands on ATLAS and not on Shamir
---------------------------------------
`Protocols/Shamir.hpp` is GRR re-sharing: `prepare_mul` does
`resharing->add_mine(x * y * rec_factor)` and `finalize` sums the received
degree-`t` shares. There is **no degree-`2t` opening anywhere in it**, so there
is nothing for a decoder to correct, and none of this applies to
`malicious-shamir-party.x`.

`Protocols/Atlas.hpp` is Damgard-Nielsen and does: `get_double_sharing()`
returns `{[r]_2t, [r]_t}`, `prepare()` sends `xy + r` to a rotating king, and
`exchange()` has the king interpolate from `2t+1` shares and re-share at
degree `t`.

What changes
------------
**The king goes.** It is where "consistent but wrong" lives: it reconstructs
from `2t+1` shares and then re-shares, and a re-sharing of the wrong value is a
perfectly consistent sharing that no amount of error correction on a codeword
will catch.

It does not have to be there. `r` is a **fresh** degree-`2t` random, so the
masked product is not secret and can go to everybody. Then every party decodes
the whole codeword for itself with Berlekamp-Welch and computes
`[xy]_t = e - [r]_t` locally, where `e` is public. Nobody's word is taken, and
there is no re-sharing step at all --- so there is nothing to segment, nothing
to check-point, and nobody to eliminate.

`Protocols/RobustDecode.h` is the decoder. It differs from
`MaliciousShamirMC::locate` in being on the common path: the honest case is one
interpolation and a comparison, and the linear system is built only once a share
is known to disagree. It returns the secret as well as the culprits, because a
robust protocol needs both halves of the answer.

The constructor refuses to start below `n >= 4t+1` rather than running with a
capacity smaller than the corruption threshold. A decoder that silently corrects
fewer liars than the protocol tolerates is the worst way for this to fail.

What it does NOT make robust
----------------------------
Only the degree-reduction step. Named honestly:

  * The **double sharings** come from `Shamir<T>::get_randoms`, which is
    hyper-invertible matrices without a malicious check in this build. A
    corrupt contributor can make `[r]_t` and `[r]_2t` inconsistent with each
    other, and no decode on either one alone sees it.
  * The **output opening** is `IndirectShamirMC`, unchanged.
  * The **inputs** are whatever the caller shared. In QOMM they are bound by
    the dealer's per-share Pedersen commitments; in this benchmark they are not.

The first of those is preprocessing, and preprocessing is allowed to abort: it
consumes no inputs, so a failed run leaks nothing and denies nobody an outcome.
It is re-run, and a node that keeps failing it is removed between auctions,
which is a governance action rather than a protocol one. Guaranteed output
delivery is only needed once real inputs are in.

Measured on host-a, n=9, T=2, 128-bit, single-phase
---------------------------------------------------
Behaviour, 2000 multiplications, `QOMM_CORRUPT_PLAYER` making the named parties
send a wrong share of every masked product (`artifacts/robust_atlas.json`):

    honest              answer correct, nobody named,      8 rounds
    corrupt {0}         answer CORRECT, named [0],         8 rounds
    corrupt {0,1}       answer CORRECT, named [0,1],       8 rounds
    corrupt {8}         answer CORRECT, named [8],         8 rounds
    corrupt {0,1,2}     refuses: "more than 2 parties sent wrong shares of a
                        masked product, which is beyond the decoding capacity
                        of a degree-4 sharing over 9 points"
    n = 7               refuses to start: "needs n >= 4t+1 ... which corrects
                        only 1"

The first four lines are the whole point: the protocol did not stop, and the
answer was right.

Cost, as a slope between 2000 and 22000 multiplications:

    ATLAS with a king, n=9      5.333 elements per party per multiplication
    this, n=9                  18.222                                  3.42x
    malicious Shamir + naming, n=7
                               64.236                                  0.28x
    rounds  this 8 -> 12,  king 9 -> 13,  malicious Shamir 11 -> 23

So against the engine actually deployed it is **3.5x cheaper per party and 2.7x
cheaper globally, with fewer rounds**, and it does not stop.

The prediction was 11.3 and it was 1.6x low
-------------------------------------------
`artifacts/robust_by_decoding_prediction.json`, written before any of this ran,
modelled the swap as "remove the king's 2 elements, add the all-to-all's `n-1`".
Measured, the swap costs 12.889 rather than 6. The model was fitted to global
traffic on the sending side only and the discrepancy has **not been isolated**,
so it is a bad model and not a finding about the protocol. What survives is the
measurement.

A native verification model of the same decode covers the capacity boundaries
in the QOMM audit tests.

